## Sex (full result, not a footnote)
- Joint Wald test on the three exposure×male interactions (MAIN varset): **F=1.431, p=0.232 — not significant.** `e_dich×male` = +0.0347 (SE 0.1221, p=0.776).
- Sex-stratified, MAIN varset:
  - MALE (n=3037): no_change +0.2052 (.1085, p=.058), took_out +0.0839 (.0887, p=.344), no_savings +0.1099 (.1154, p=.341), e_dich +0.0214 (.0742, p=.773)
  - FEMALE (n=3011): no_change −0.0532 (.1494, p=.722), took_out +0.0767 (.1219, p=.530), no_savings −0.1190 (.1507, p=.430), e_dich +0.0198 (.1002, p=.843)
- In the sex-stratified complete-case ladder (results_sexstrat.csv), spec d (+income89, n=2457 men): MALE no_change +0.239 (p=.047) and no_savings +0.256 (p=.047); FEMALE (n=2529) all coefficients negative or null. The only nominally significant cell-level estimates in the whole analysis are these two male coefficients plus one pooled spec-a dummy (e_no_change +0.173, p=.029, n=8434); they are non-monotone across categories — the opposite of the descriptive hint in the planning round (which was driven by sex imbalance in baseline BMI, not by an exposure gradient). We do **not** claim a sex difference: the interaction test is null and one significant cell in ~28 tests is expected by chance.

## Robustness and selection
- **MICE (m=5, covariates only — never exposure, never outcome):** e_no_change +0.1555 (SE .080, p=.053), e_took_out +0.0286 (p=.672), e_no_savings +0.0696 (p=.392). Still no monotone pattern; conclusions unchanged.
- **Exposure proxy B** (savings ordinal + low wealth growth, the index the researcher rejected for main): +0.0283 (SE .0419, p=.499) with full controls, +0.0181 (p=.631) without wealth terms. Null either way, and corr(exposure, −wealth growth)=0.104, so the composite added almost no independent signal.
- **Secondary outcome, person-mean BMI over observed waves** (≥2 waves): raw associations are large and significant (+0.46/+0.41/+0.67 BMI units, all p<.001) but collapse to +0.013/+0.009/+0.002 (all p>.76) once background and wealth85 are included.
- **Within-person FE:** exposure is time-invariant, so demeaning removes it exactly — coefficients are identically 0, R²=0. FE carries no information here and is reported only to close it out.
- **Selection check 1, P(ΔBMI unobserved) ~ exposure + bmi81 + demog + wealth85:** e_no_change +0.004 (p=.734), e_took_out +0.011 (p=.291), **e_no_savings +0.077 (SE .011, p<.0001)**. Attrition into 1992 is 18.9%/17.7%/19.9%/**22.0%** for put-in/took-out/no-change/**no-savings**. Differential loss of the "no savings" group would, if anything, bias the estimated positive gradient *downward*, i.e. toward our null.
- **Selection check 2, P(exposure unobserved):** 3.2% among those with BMI-1992 vs **46.8%** among those without. It is *not* predicted by BMI or demographics (bmi81 p=.28, bmi89 p=.97, male p=.59, age p=.23) — it is joint wave non-response, so the 45.6%-vs-2.6% gap reflects survey participation, not health-related selection on the exposure.

## Effect-size ceiling
MAIN-spec 95% CIs, expressed in SD of ΔBMI (sd=2.475): e_no_change [−0.037, +0.106] SD,
e_took_out [−0.026, +0.092] SD, e_no_savings [−0.073, +0.076] SD. We can rule out any effect
above ≈**0.1 SD (0.25 BMI units) over three years** for any savings category.

## Limitations
1. Time preference is **proxied**, not measured; the proxy is a household-level, single-wave,
   self-reported savings-direction item.
2. "No savings" **confounds impatience with poverty** (40.2% hold non-positive wealth; ASVAB z −0.52).
   Controlling for SES removes the exposure gradient, and we cannot separate "preference" from
   "capacity" with these variables.
3. Exposure is measured at the same interview as the baseline outcome (1989), so reverse
   causality within 1989 cannot be excluded; the ΔBMI design mitigates but does not remove it.
4. Height for 1989/1992 is carried forward from 1985 (no height in those waves), so BMI levels
   carry a small classical-measurement error; the carry-forward is validated (median |Δ| 0.0 in,
   94.2% within 2 in vs 1981).
5. Complete-case n=6048 (47.7%) and the fixed-sample n=4986 (39.3%) — attrition is dominated by
   wave non-response, and the exposure-missingness pattern is not BMI-related.
6. R² of the MAIN spec is 0.011: this model explains almost none of BMI change. Absence of a
   detected effect here is **not** evidence that time preference is unrelated to BMI; it is
   evidence that this proxy, in this cohort, over this 3-year window, shows no detectable
   association with weight change once background is held fixed.
7. No causal interpretation is supported. Everything above is associational in an
   observational cohort with a measured-at-one-wave proxy exposure.
